WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.199
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.499 --> 00:00:08.549
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.849 --> 00:00:15.960
Unit nine: Executive Communication. Today you will describe communicative balance with 'balanced with' structures.

4
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.947
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:22.947
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.783 --> 00:00:25.481
Executive presence.

7
00:00:26.483 --> 00:00:31.339
Executive presence means the capacity to inspire confidence through composure and authority.

8
00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:35.946
Her executive presence steadied the room during the crisis briefing.

9
00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:40.027
Your turn. Say: Executive presence.

10
00:00:42.867 --> 00:00:44.344
Executive presence.

11
00:00:45.083 --> 00:00:46.468
Concise messaging.

12
00:00:47.483 --> 00:00:51.840
Concise messaging means communicating key points in as few words as possible.

13
00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:55.611
Concise messaging kept the board's attention for the full hour.

14
00:00:56.217 --> 00:00:59.368
Your turn. Say: Concise messaging.

15
00:01:02.233 --> 00:01:03.757
Concise messaging.

16
00:01:04.517 --> 00:01:05.634
Stakeholder.

17
00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:11.610
Stakeholder means a person or group with an interest in an organisation's decisions.

18
00:01:12.217 --> 00:01:15.366
Every stakeholder received a tailored summary of the merger.

19
00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:19.032
Your turn. Say: Stakeholder.

20
00:01:21.867 --> 00:01:22.845
Stakeholder.

21
00:01:23.583 --> 00:01:24.457
Briefing.

22
00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:29.423
Briefing means a short meeting or document giving essential information.

23
00:01:30.017 --> 00:01:33.224
The minister requested a two-page briefing before the summit.

24
00:01:33.817 --> 00:01:36.074
Your turn. Say: Briefing.

25
00:01:38.933 --> 00:01:39.969
Briefing.

26
00:01:40.717 --> 00:01:41.660
Persuasion.

27
00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:46.426
Persuasion means the act of convincing others to accept an idea.

28
00:01:47.017 --> 00:01:50.189
Persuasion without evidence quickly erodes credibility.

29
00:01:50.783 --> 00:01:53.296
Your turn. Say: Persuasion.

30
00:01:56.133 --> 00:01:57.146
Persuasion.

31
00:01:57.883 --> 00:01:58.826
Clarity.

32
00:01:59.817 --> 00:02:03.082
Clarity means the quality of being easy to understand.

33
00:02:03.683 --> 00:02:06.658
Clarity matters more than eloquence in a crisis.

34
00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:09.600
Your turn. Say: Clarity.

35
00:02:12.467 --> 00:02:13.398
Clarity.

36
00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:15.128
Authority.

37
00:02:16.117 --> 00:02:20.043
Authority means the power to command respect and influence decisions.

38
00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:23.230
Authority comes from competence, not volume.

39
00:02:23.817 --> 00:02:26.051
Your turn. Say: Authority.

40
00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:29.820
Authority.

41
00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:31.735
Influence.

42
00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:36.688
Influence means the capacity to shape others' thinking or behaviour.

43
00:02:37.283 --> 00:02:40.397
Quiet influence often outlasts loud command.

44
00:02:40.983 --> 00:02:43.566
Your turn. Say: Influence.

45
00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:47.482
Influence.

46
00:02:48.267 --> 00:02:51.883
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:52.717 --> 00:02:58.144
Visal, the new CEO speaks with total certainty, yet staff seem to resent her. Why?

48
00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:07.789
Because her confidence isn't balanced with openness. Certainty without curiosity is often perceived as arrogance.

49
00:03:08.883 --> 00:03:10.778
But shouldn't leaders project strength?

50
00:03:11.883 --> 00:03:22.187
Yes, but strength is most credible when it's tempered by humility. Admitting what you don't know actually reinforces trust in what you do know.

51
00:03:23.283 --> 00:03:25.132
So what would you advise her?

52
00:03:26.217 --> 00:03:34.268
Pair every decisive message with a genuine question. Directness balanced with respect sounds authoritative rather than domineering.

53
00:03:35.433 --> 00:03:37.061
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:37.917 --> 00:03:41.867
Grammar focus: Balance and contrast structures (balanced with).

55
00:03:42.167 --> 00:03:43.342
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:47.489
Authority is balanced with clarity in strong communication.

57
00:03:48.383 --> 00:03:51.103
Her confidence is tempered by genuine humility.

58
00:03:52.017 --> 00:03:54.922
Directness balanced with respect sounds authoritative.

59
00:03:55.867 --> 00:04:06.602
'Balanced with', 'tempered by' and 'offset by' signal that one quality limits or completes another. They can be reduced to participle phrases: 'Directness balanced with respect sounds authoritative.'

60
00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:10.943
Be careful! Don't say: Authority is balanced to clarity.

61
00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.457
Say: Authority is balanced with clarity.

62
00:04:15.757 --> 00:04:17.222
Balance X with Y.

63
00:04:18.033 --> 00:04:20.593
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:22.854
Speaking With Authority.

65
00:04:23.154 --> 00:04:30.099
Executive communication differs from everyday communication in that every word may affect markets, morale and reputations.

66
00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:37.999
Research on leadership suggests that audiences grant authority to speakers whose confidence is balanced with visible competence and respect.

67
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:45.922
Concise messaging is essential, since senior stakeholders typically decide within minutes whether a briefing merits further attention.

68
00:04:46.933 --> 00:04:53.298
Yet brevity must be tempered by context, because oversimplification can be as damaging as verbosity.

69
00:04:54.300 --> 00:05:01.094
The most influential executives combine clarity, candour and calm, projecting authority without resorting to arrogance.

70
00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:06.213
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:09.363
Her ambition is balanced blank patience.

72
00:05:09.663 --> 00:05:12.655
A, to. B, with. C, for.

73
00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:17.816
The answer is B: with.

74
00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:22.250
A short document giving essential information is a blank.

75
00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:26.308
A, briefing. B, stakeholder. C, clarity.

76
00:05:29.867 --> 00:05:31.762
The answer is A: briefing.

77
00:05:32.917 --> 00:05:35.659
The capacity to shape others' thinking is blank.

78
00:05:35.959 --> 00:05:40.541
A, concise messaging. B, persuasion. C, influence.

79
00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.518
The answer is C: influence.

80
00:05:47.683 --> 00:05:49.497
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:49.797 --> 00:05:56.922
A, His certainty was tempered with by doubt.. B, His certainty was tempered by doubt.. C, His certainty tempered to doubt..

82
00:06:00.467 --> 00:06:03.743
The answer is B: His certainty was tempered by doubt..

83
00:06:04.883 --> 00:06:09.205
According to the reading, authority is granted to speakers whose confidence is…

84
00:06:09.505 --> 00:06:15.120
A, balanced with competence and respect. B, louder than others'. C, never questioned.

85
00:06:18.667 --> 00:06:22.059
The answer is A: balanced with competence and respect.

86
00:06:23.267 --> 00:06:26.523
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:27.383 --> 00:06:30.881
What makes communication feel authoritative without being arrogant?

88
00:06:47.683 --> 00:06:49.218
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:49.518 --> 00:07:24.765
Communication feels authoritative when confidence is balanced with genuine respect for the audience. An authoritative speaker states conclusions clearly, supports them with evidence and accepts responsibility, yet remains open to questions. Arrogance appears when certainty is not tempered by humility, for instance when a leader dismisses concerns instead of addressing them. Paradoxically, admitting the limits of one's knowledge often increases credibility, because listeners trust honesty more than performance.

90
00:07:25.065 --> 00:07:27.030
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:27.917 --> 00:07:33.263
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and balance and contrast structures (balanced with).

92
00:07:33.563 --> 00:07:38.467
Today's value is leadership. Lead fairly, listen to others and set a good example.

93
00:07:38.767 --> 00:07:41.152
See you in Unit ten. Goodbye!

94
00:07:41.452 --> 00:07:44.488
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

